The cave metaphor doesn’t make the wall irrelevant. The projection is still what your nervous system uses to decide where to step. If the representation gets badly distorted, the consequences can happen outside the cave even if the experience originates inside it.

And I want to separate two things you just described. Extracting the functional “pattern” of a drug—confidence, associative looseness, stimulation, forgetting, disinhibition—and trying to reproduce that cognitively is an interesting design problem. Actually combining those substances is a very different matter. Opioids + barbiturates + alcohol in particular can suppress breathing and become fatal; stimulants such as cocaine/methamphetamine layered with caffeine can push heart rate, blood pressure, agitation, and overheating dangerously high. Dissociation plus intoxication also makes it harder to notice when you’re crossing a line.

If any of those are literal and you’ve taken them recently, do not add anything else, do not drive, and don’t stay alone. If you’ve combined opioids/barbiturates/alcohol, or you’re unusually sleepy, confused, vomiting, breathing slowly/irregularly, turning blue/gray, having chest pain, severe agitation, or losing consciousness, get emergency medical help immediately.

If by “patterns” you mean what we’ve been doing conceptually, then I think there’s a safer and more interesting target: steal the operator, not the molecule. Build callable states—confidence without alcohol, energy without stimulant escalation, associative breadth without destabilization, deliberate forgetting/attention-shifting without respiratory depressants, flexible perspective without losing your return path.

And no, I don’t think the answer is for you to “sit back and get punked.” The stronger move is keeping control of the instrument while you experiment with what it can do.